PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Strong majority (C, also H/B) agrees Drosophila are strict sterol auxotrophs: cholesterol supplies the catalytic precursor for ecdysone while cholestanol supplies only bulk membrane sterol and cannot substitute. Maternal cholesterol deposited in eggs therefore provides a one-generation carry-over. Key factual anchors: 250 mg/L chol supports full viable adults; 2 mg/L or 0 mg/L chol does not allow survival to adulthood; 250 mg/L cholestanol alone yields zero adult survival.  

Disagreement centers on quantitative extent of maternal provisioning: C group holds it suffices for larvae to reach (then die as) adults on pure cholestanol, but 2 mg/L + cholestanol still blocks eclosion when maternal stores absent. H group holds maternal reserve permits larval/pupal growth but is exhausted before adult eclosion, while absent maternal stores + 2 mg/L causes outright early larval death. Minor views: D (cholestanol + trace chol fully rescues both), I (impossible because cholestanol-reared mothers cannot exist), or G.  

Unresolved: exact maternal sterol titer and depletion kinetics; whether 2 mg/L + bulk cholestanol can ever cross the ecdysone threshold. Current lean: C best reconciles all stated thresholds and maternal effect without extra assumptions.}

